# Target C-II — optimized three-cut kernel has at most `1085` exceptional diameter rows

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only quantitative strengthening of the exact three-cut kernel  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** exact three-cut kernel/cusp theorem; C65 full actual-diameter visibility correction; audited `0.7404D` weighted smallest-leaf fan; `t<=1.844` retained dominant mass `z>2.2069`; Section-171 residual envelope; exact laminar interval support  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_three_cut_kernel_cusp_1086.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The exact three-cut kernel theorem localizes every nonzero first derivative to the union
of the switching rows at two adjacent backbone boundaries.  Its original extraction
used the older all-purpose triple-small parameters and left

\[
\#H_{exc}<42400+o(1).
\]

Two later inputs make that count obsolete:

1. the `t<=1.844` retained-mass/defect ledger bounds the full actual-diameter edge
   density by
   \[
   {h_H\over D}<1.851259926+o(1),
   \]
   so the global interval-switch count satisfies
   \[
   \sum_i N_i<3.702519852D+o(D);
   \]
2. for a count-focused three-cut extraction, the high-center split can be pushed close
   to the lower-degree phase boundary.  The resulting good-leaf backbone has density
   \[
   p=0.0068224645\ldots,
   \]
   almost twice the path density used in the original three-cut theorem.

Combining those two facts with a Pareto union bound gives a three-cut kernel whose
**entire exceptional actual-diameter set has at most `1085` rows**, while the total
exceptional KKT mass and the total adjacent phase variation remain `O(D^{-1})`.

More precisely, every sufficiently large survivor in the stated scope contains three
consecutive smallest-leaf O--O cuts

\[
\tau_1,\tau_2,\tau_3
\]

and a nonzero normalized kernel vector

\[
c=(c_1,c_2,c_3),
\qquad
c_1u_1+c_2u_2+c_3u_3=0,
\qquad
\max_j|c_j|=1,
\tag{K1086.1}
\]

such that, for the exact laminar multishear `theta_j=t c_j`:

\[
\boxed{|H_{exc}|\le1085,}
\tag{K1086.2}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\in H_{exc}}\beta_h
<{10^{14}\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
}
\tag{K1086.3}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
 d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_1,u_2)
 +d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_2,u_3)
<{10^{14}\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{K1086.4}

Every actual diameter row outside `H_exc` has zero first squared-length derivative
**exactly**, as in the parent kernel theorem.  C65 makes every actual diameter row
force-visible, so the exceptional set contains positive-stress rows with both derivative
signs.  If the three train directions span the plane, the parent implicit-function
argument integrates the kernel to a genuine finite contact-preserving motion under
which every nonexceptional diameter row is unchanged exactly.

The constants `10^14` in (K1086.3)--(K1086.4) are intentionally nonoptimized.  They
only preserve the inverse-`D` scale while the count threshold is optimized.  The
load-bearing gain is `42400 -> 1085`.

---

## 1. Sharper actual-diameter density on the full `t<=1.844` branch

Use the current matched atomic constant

\[
C_{707}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.007707,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

Write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}.
\]

The Section-171 residual envelope is

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}+h_{res}
={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{707},
\qquad
h_{res}\ge0.
\tag{K1086.5}

The uniform `t<=1.844` retained dominant-occurrence theorem gives

\[
z>2.2069-o(1),
\]

and the one-orbit energy floor gives

\[
\delta>F(2.2069)-o(1),
\qquad
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6).
\tag{K1086.6}

At the adverse endpoint `t=1.844`, substituting (K1086.6) into (K1086.5) gives the
already-certified bound

\[
\boxed{\kappa<0.007259926+o(1).}
\tag{K1086.7}

The exact topology identity

\[
q-k=h_H-s_Z,
\qquad
s_Z\le k,
\]

therefore yields

\[
\boxed{
{h_H\over D}
=t+{s_Z\over D}
\le t+\kappa
<1.851259926+o(1).
}
\tag{K1086.8}

For any laminar backbone path, each actual diameter interval starts and stops at most
once.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_i N_i
\le2h_H
<3.702519852D+o(D).
}
\tag{K1086.9}

This is the count budget used below.

---

## 2. Count-optimized good-leaf backbone

Use the audited weighted fan inputs

\[
{\sum_\tau w_\tau d_\tau\over\sum_\tau w_\tau}
>0.7404-o(1),
\tag{K1086.10}
\]

\[
{1\over D}\sum_\tau w_\tau>0.0848-o(1),
\qquad
N_C<1.063D+o(D).
\tag{K1086.11}

Choose

\[
\lambda={1601\over2500}=0.6404
\tag{K1086.12}
\]

and call a retained smallest-leaf O--O train good when its center degree exceeds
`lambda D`.

Weighted thresholding gives retained good weight, hence also distinct good-leaf count
density,

\[
\boxed{
g
={ (0.7404-\lambda)(0.0848)\over1.063-\lambda}
=0.0200662565\ldots .
}
\tag{K1086.13}

For the high-center split, keep a small positive lower-degree reserve

\[
d_{low}:={1\over10000}.
\tag{K1086.14}

Set

\[
r:={\lambda-d_{low}\over1.063}
=0.6023518344\ldots .
\tag{K1086.15}

Thus a good leaf has at least `d_low D-o(D)` center neighbors below any center train in
the top `r` phase fraction, while its number of neighbors inside that top fraction is
at least

\[
\boxed{
d_{top}
:=\lambda-(1-r)(1.063)
=0.2177.
}
\tag{K1086.16}

Double-count good-leaf incidences with the top `r` center block.  Some center train
`gamma` crosses a simple dual-tree path of distinct good leaves of length

\[
\boxed{
m>pD-o(D),}
\tag{K1086.17}
\]

where

\[
\boxed{
p
:={d_{top}g\over r(1.063)}
=0.0068224645348\ldots .
}
\tag{K1086.18}

This is the same safe high-center/path extraction used in the adjacent count
optimization; no unweighted fan normalization is introduced.

---

## 3. Preserve the phase budget despite the count optimization

Let `alpha_gamma` be the chosen center phase and `beta_j` the real phase of the `j`th
leaf on its path.  Put

\[
q_j:=\beta_j-\alpha_\gamma-\pi/3>0.
\]

Each path leaf has at least `d_low D-o(D)` center neighbors of phase at most
`alpha_gamma`.  Every such strict-rhombus crossing pays angular defect at least `q_j`.
The global strict-rhombus angular-defect bound therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=1}^m q_j
\le {C_{phase}\over d_{low}}+o(1),
}
\tag{K1086.19}

with

\[
C_{phase}={\pi^2\sqrt3(2+\sqrt3)\over12}.
\]

Consequently the total two-boundary phase cost over all consecutive triples satisfies

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{m-2}
D[(q_i+q_{i+1})+(q_{i+1}+q_{i+2})]
\le {4C_{phase}\over d_{low}}D+o(D).
\tag{K1086.20}

The constant is large because `d_low=10^-4`; it is still finite and therefore retains
the required inverse-`D` phase scale after the Pareto extraction.

---

## 4. Two-boundary count, KKT, and phase Pareto extraction

For one internal boundary let

\[
N_i:=\#\{h:\varepsilon_{h,i+1}\ne\varepsilon_{hi}\},
\]

and

\[
B_i:=\sum_h\beta_h
|\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}|.
\]

The exact interval variation gives

\[
\sum_iB_i\le2.
\tag{K1086.21}

For a triple start `i`, define

\[
\mathcal N_i:=N_i+N_{i+1},
\]

\[
\mathcal B_i:=D(B_i+B_{i+1}),
\]

and

\[
\mathcal P_i:=D[(q_i+q_{i+1})+(q_{i+1}+q_{i+2})].
\tag{K1086.22}

Each internal boundary occurs in at most two consecutive triples.  Therefore

\[
\sum_i\mathcal N_i
\le2\sum_jN_j
<7.405039704D+o(D),
\tag{K1086.23}
\]

\[
\sum_i\mathcal B_i
\le4D,
\tag{K1086.24}
\]

and (K1086.20) controls the phase sum.

Take the fixed thresholds

\[
N_*:=1086,
\qquad
L:=10^{14}.
\tag{K1086.25}

The density of triple starts violating `mathcal N_i<N_*` is at most

\[
{2(3.702519852)\over1086}+o(1)
=0.006818637\ldots+o(1).
\tag{K1086.26}

The density violating `mathcal B_i<L` is at most

\[
{4\over L},
\tag{K1086.27}

and the density violating `mathcal P_i<L` is at most

\[
{4C_{phase}\over d_{low}L}+o(1).
\tag{K1086.28}

The exact companion certificate, using the standard outward enclosures for `pi`,
`sqrt(3)`, and `F(2.2069)`, proves

\[
\boxed{
p-
{2(3.702519852)\over1086}
-{4\over10^{14}}
-{4C_{phase}\over10^{-4}10^{14}}
>3.82\times10^{-6}.
}
\tag{K1086.29}

Hence at least one consecutive triple is good for all three thresholds.

For that triple,

\[
\mathcal N_i<1086+o(1).
\]

Since `mathcal N_i` is integer, sufficiently far along the survivor sequence

\[
\boxed{\mathcal N_i\le1085.}
\tag{K1086.30}

Also

\[
\boxed{B_i+B_{i+1}<{10^{14}\over D}+o(D^{-1}),}
\tag{K1086.31}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
(q_i+q_{i+1})+(q_{i+1}+q_{i+2})
<{10^{14}\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{K1086.32}

---

## 5. Insert the optimized triple into the exact kernel theorem

Relabel the selected triple as `tau_1,tau_2,tau_3`.  The exact three-cut theorem chooses
`c!=0` with

\[
c_1u_1+c_2u_2+c_3u_3=0.
\]

By interval support, every diameter row with nonzero first derivative belongs to

\[
H_{exc}:=H_{sw,1}\cup H_{sw,2}.
\]

Therefore

\[
|H_{exc}|
\le N_i+N_{i+1}
\le1085,
\]

proving (K1086.2).

A row in the union contributes its positive KKT multiplier at least once to
`B_i+B_{i+1}`, so

\[
\sum_{h\in H_{exc}}\beta_h
\le B_i+B_{i+1},
\]

which gives (K1086.3).

The two adjacent projective leaf gaps are bounded by `q_i+q_{i+1}` and
`q_{i+1}+q_{i+2}`, proving (K1086.4).

All exact conclusions of the parent kernel theorem now apply unchanged:

- nonexceptional diameter rows have exactly zero first derivative;
- KKT stationarity is supported entirely on `H_exc`;
- mixed rigidity makes the exceptional cusp nontrivial;
- canonical C65 forces actual positive-stress exceptional rows of both derivative
  signs;
- in the nonparallel case, the implicit-function kernel integrates to an exact finite
  motion leaving every nonexceptional diameter row unchanged exactly.

The later adjacent-strip theorems add further structure boundary-by-boundary: each of
the two switching graphs is an ordered caterpillar forest, and each nonzero adjacent
KKT layer reaction has a one/two-diameter conic certificate.  Those facts do not by
themselves remove the three-cut sign cusp, but they now act on a union of at most
`1085` actual rows rather than `42400+o(1)`.

---

## 6. Frontier

The live three-cut obstruction is now

```text
three consecutive smallest-leaf O-O cuts
+ exact planar shear kernel c1 u1 + c2 u2 + c3 u3 = 0
+ <=1085 actual exceptional diameter rows
+ total exceptional beta mass O(1/D)
+ total adjacent phase variation O(1/D)
+ every nonexceptional diameter row first-order silent exactly
+ C65-positive exceptional rows of both derivative signs
+ each adjacent switching graph has prefix/hub/suffix caterpillar order
+ each nonzero adjacent KKT reaction is generated by <=2 switching diameter bars.
```

This is still not a diameter-decreasing motion.  Small total KKT mass does not weaken an
individual max constraint, and the positive-stress sign cusp remains load-bearing.
The remaining problem is finite/order-geometric: combine the two adjacent ordered
interfaces and their sparse KKT reactions, or construct a finite exchange that defeats
the residual `<=1085`-row cusp.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only quantitative strengthening of the exact three-cut
kernel.  C-II and Level I remain open.
